<http://data.silknow.org/event/f3468a78-0356-3bf5-9af8-6ad8e20863ba>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "David Fraser identified these garments as two halves of a blouse pointing out that: \n1. the centre stitching had been cut\n2. weaving of the field corresponds\n3. Supplementary weft patterns across back and chest match. \n\nHe further believes this to be the oldest documented Chin woman's tunic."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/da202cee-bdac-3336-931e-d2c41a7aba06> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "David Fraser identified these garments as two halves of a blouse pointing out that: \n1. the centre stitching had been cut\n2. weaving of the field corresponds\n3. Supplementary weft patterns across back and chest match. \n\nHe further believes this to be the oldest documented Chin woman's tunic."@en .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/da202cee-bdac-3336-931e-d2c41a7aba06/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "To reform the blouse the two parallel rectangular lengths of cloth would be sewn together with holes left for the head, arms and front opening. Said to be traditionally sewn on the back and left open in front. Latterly for the sake of modesty the front was pinned.\n\nBlack ground with an elaborate band of yellow silk thread geometric patterns in a supplementary weave across the chest and back. This band is bisected by a  narrow row of stripes of yellow silk thread over each shoulder and down the front and back. Further decorated with yellow silk thread supplementary weave on the weft edges."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/da202cee-bdac-3336-931e-d2c41a7aba06> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/da202cee-bdac-3336-931e-d2c41a7aba06>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Two halves of a Chin woman's blouse from Rakhine (Arracan) State, Burma. Black cotton with geometric patterns in yellow silk thread supplementary weave. c. 1855."@en , "To reform the blouse the two parallel rectangular lengths of cloth would be sewn together with holes left for the head, arms and front opening. Said to be traditionally sewn on the back and left open in front. Latterly for the sake of modesty the front was pinned.\n\nBlack ground with an elaborate band of yellow silk thread geometric patterns in a supplementary weave across the chest and back. This band is bisected by a  narrow row of stripes of yellow silk thread over each shoulder and down the front and back. Further decorated with yellow silk thread supplementary weave on the weft edges."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/db3f2969-2641-3755-985f-dca378a62898> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Two halves of a Chin woman's blouse from Rakhine (Arracan) State, Burma. Black cotton with geometric patterns in yellow silk thread supplementary weave. c. 1855."@en , "To reform the blouse the two parallel rectangular lengths of cloth would be sewn together with holes left for the head, arms and front opening. Said to be traditionally sewn on the back and left open in front. Latterly for the sake of modesty the front was pinned.\n\nBlack ground with an elaborate band of yellow silk thread geometric patterns in a supplementary weave across the chest and back. This band is bisected by a  narrow row of stripes of yellow silk thread over each shoulder and down the front and back. Further decorated with yellow silk thread supplementary weave on the weft edges."@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "5624(IS)" ;
